      "post_text": "Twelve users. Three months of engineering. Four weeks before we pulled the plug.\n\nWe were convinced the feature made sense. Users had complained about the workflow, we had the bandwidth, and the team was excited. That felt like enough.\n\nIt wasn't.\n\nWe shipped in week one. By week three, I was staring at the dashboard at 10pm, watching the same 12 accounts bounce in and out. Some of them were us, testing our own product.\n\nThe uncomfortable truth: we had confused \"users complaining about a problem\" with \"users willing to change their behavior to solve it.\" Those are very different things, and I should have known better.\n\nWhat we do now before a single line gets written:\n\n– Find five people who say they have the problem\n– Ask them what they currently do instead\n– Put a rough prototype in front of them and watch, not explain\n\nThat last step is the one most teams skip. Watching someone use a thing you built, in silence, while they struggle, is brutal. It's also the only feedback that doesn't lie.\n\nWe probably still get this wrong sometimes. But 3 months and 12 users was enough to change how we build permanently.\n\nWhat does your team do before writing code?"
